<header><p class="st-kicker">prince bishopric · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Imperial Abbey of Weingarten</h1><p class="st-meta">1274 CE – 1803 CE (approx.) · capital Weingarten</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Benedictine abbey founded by the Guelphs that guarded a relic of the Holy Blood and built the largest baroque church in Germany.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-welf">Guelph</a> family founded a convent at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weingarten-wurttemberg">Altdorf</a> near <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-ravensburg">Ravensburg</a> in the 11th century and in 1056 replaced it with a Benedictine abbey that became their family monastery. In 1094 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judith-of-flanders">Judith of Flanders</a>, wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/welf-i-duke-of-bavaria">Welf IV</a>, gave it a relic of the Holy Blood. After the Guelph and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Hohenstaufen</a> lines passed, the abbey became imperial, by around 1274.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The abbots ruled villages and farmland in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-swabia">Upper Swabia</a>, sat among the Swabian imperial prelates and were among the richest in the region. The Holy Blood relic drew pilgrims, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/blutritt">Blutritt</a> horseback procession continues today. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weingarten-wurttemberg">Weingarten</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> of medieval German love songs was kept there. From 1715 to 1724 the abbey built its vast baroque basilica.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The abbey was secularised in 1803, passing to the house of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-nassau-diez">Nassau-Orange</a> and in 1806 to Württemberg. Benedictine monks returned in the 20th century, and the basilica remains a major pilgrimage church.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/imperial-abbey-of-weingarten.jpg" alt="Imperial Abbey of Weingarten" width="320" height="210"><figcaption>Image: Wikimedia Commons · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Weingarten_Kloster_1500.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1274 CE – 1803 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>prince bishopric</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Weingarten</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Swabian German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Weingarten Abbey</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1538&amp;focus=imperial-abbey-of-weingarten" aria-label="Imperial Abbey of Weingarten on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4905 503 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Imperial Abbey of Weingarten lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5225 759L5229 694M5225 759L5183 683"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5183 683h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5225 759h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5225 759h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Weingarten. <a href="/atlas?year=1538&amp;focus=imperial-abbey-of-weingarten">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
